.TH NEWSTEST 1
.SH NAME
newstest \- regression test for news(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B newstest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Newstest
loads
.BR /p9/dis/news.dis 's
in-process test seam,
.BR run(args) ,
and checks its returned output string against hand-traced expectations
- there is no independent oracle binary built here (unlike
.IR cbtest (1)
or
.IR qrtest (1)):
.IR news (1)'s
own behaviour is tied tightly enough to real host directory
modification times and
.B $home
that hand-tracing the real algorithm on a controlled fixture is the
actual verification, not a difference from this backlog's usual style.
.PP
Because
.IR news (1)
has no flag to point
.B /lib/news
or
.B $home/lib/newstime
anywhere but their real, hardcoded paths (see
.IR news (1)'s
own BUGS), this suite creates and removes a real directory at
.B /lib/news
and a real per-user marker under a
.B $home
it points at itself via
.BR Env->setenv ,
restoring whatever
.B $home
this process had beforehand when done.
.B refuseifexists()
checks both paths before touching either, and refuses to run at all if
something is already there, rather than silently overwriting real
content that might belong to something else on this host.
.SH CASES
.TP
.I t0
.B /lib/news
missing entirely fails cleanly, checked before the fixture directory
is created.
.TP
.I t1
With no
.B $home
set at all, every non-ignored item prints, newest first, every time -
there is no way to persist a "last read" mark without one. Includes a
hand-traced check of the body-text tab-reindentation and blank-line
collapsing (see BUGS).
.TP
.I t2 / t3 / t3b / t4-t6
With
.B $home
set: the first run (no marker file yet) prints everything and writes
the marker; an immediate second run sees nothing new.
.I t3b
checks something an advisor review flagged as unverified: that
run()'s marker write - a plain
.B sys->create
on a file that, by
.IR t3 ,
already exists - actually advances its modification time rather than
silently no-op'ing on an existing file. Checked directly by
.BR sys->stat -ing
the marker before and after a further call, not assumed; a negative
control (forcing the marker write to skip files that already exist)
confirmed this specific assertion catches it - the marker stops
advancing and the very next default call wrongly shows nothing new
forever, the real-world symptom this would cause.
.PP
After a new item is added,
.B -n
and
.B -a
are each checked twice in a row, before any boundary-moving call is
ever made, to confirm neither one moves the "last read" mark by
itself; only then does a plain default call (the one real
.I news
itself would make) consume the new item, and a further default call
afterward confirms it is now gone.
.TP
.I t7
Explicit named file arguments print in the order given, not
modification-time order, and bypass the boundary/ignore-list logic
entirely.
.TP
.I t8
An unknown flag fails cleanly.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/newstest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR news (1)
.SH BUGS
Does not exercise multiple distinct
.B $home
users concurrently, or any permission-denied path on
.B /lib/news
or the marker file - this host's filesystem backend does not make
either easy to force reliably.
.PP
Relies on a real
.B sys->sleep
between fixture files (not a busy loop) to guarantee strictly
increasing modification times, since this host's own modification-time
granularity is not assumed finer than one second; this makes the suite
take several real seconds to run, not a fast unit test.
.PP
Does not exercise
.BR news.b 's
own date formatting on a single-digit day of month (asctime's
space-padding, as opposed to zero-padding, only shows up then) - this
suite's own fixture files are all created "today", so whether that
falls on such a day depends on when the suite is run. Checked
directly instead, once, with a standalone probe printing
.B %2d
and
.B %.2d
against known values (space-pads and zero-pads respectively, as
expected) rather than encoded as a fragile date-dependent case here.
.PP
The body-text hand-trace
.RB ( t1 )
covers exactly one input shape (a run of three consecutive blank
lines collapsing to one) - not every bol/bop transition the real
algorithm can reach.
